window.docContents[28].resolve({"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Metrology-on-a-Verified-Numerical-Framework--The-trust-chain___-link-by-link":{"contents":"From a kinded source to bits on a device, each link is either a theorem or a named trusted base.\nEvery theorem cited here depends on propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound only;\nMatch.isBisimulation_contracted depends on propext and Quot.sound.\n\n* Link* Carried by* Trusted\n* Kinded source to the erased kernel* @[simp] erasure lemmas proved by rfl (Quantity.add_magnitude, …): the naked kernel is the kinded one with .magnitude applied* nothing\n* Erased kernel to the binary32 rungs* Quantity.add_refines at the rounding specification, unconditionally; Quantity.add_refines_exec at the executable binary32, under the explicit hypothesis that the sum is finite* nothing; overflow is a hypothesis, not an assumption\n* Kernel to its tape* the denotation bridge (TapeParity.Evaluates) and its homomorphism Evaluates.isNumCarrierHom, from TorchLean's per-operator Tape.apply correctness* nothing\n* Tape to compacted tape to printed program* pipeline_denotes: at every environment, the program printed from the compacted tape holds at the remapped slot of every original node the value the original tape computes there; SL.genSL_denotes for the program alone, table fetches included* nothing, up to the program; the printer's dozen forms meaning in the host language what they mean in Lean's Float\n* Tape graph to provenance hypergraph* Match.isWeakBisimulation_weak, and Match.isBisimulation_contracted on the contracted tape graph; the seal of the computation (TapeSeal.semantic_seal), carried to the printed program by semantic_seal_program* the harvest's reading of the source, which the bisimulation cross-checks rather than trusts\n* Printed program to device* for a table fetch, TorchLean's CUDA.denotes_emit: the generated CUDA source denotes the kernel expression Program.of certified against its Lean source* the host compiler with contraction and reassociation disabled; the host's IEEE conformance; exp and log from glibc, libdevice or Julia's Base.Math, checked by the parity executables to an ulp bound\n\n\n\nThe executable binary32 rung, with overflow stated. For kind-k quantities carried at\nthe executable binary32 (FloatLib's ExecFloat.Binary 8 23, on which TorchLean builds), if the executable sum is finite, forgetting it to ℝ gives the\nbinary32 rounding of the real sum. Where the hypothesis fails (an overflow to infinity, a NaN\noperand) the theorem claims nothing: it proves the equality on the finite path, not its failure off\nit, and the hypothesis marks that boundary.\n\nFloatLib's ExecFloat.Binary.toReal_add_eq_roundAt, transported along the kind index: the kinded sum is the\nnaked sum under Quantity.mk.\n\nThe denotation bridge is a homomorphism. The bridge relates both leaf formers and each of +,\n−, ×, ÷, min, max, sqrt and exp on the recording carrier to the matching\nelementwise operation on tensors, bundled as one record. The tape_hom tactic closes a model's\nparity by unfolding the model's own definitions and walking the exposed operation tree with\nthese cases.\n\nEach field is one of the per-operation preservation lemmas of the denotation bridge, which are\nproved from the tape's forward-value faithfulness lemmas through a run bridge.\n\nTape, compacted tape and program agree. For every well-formed tape without table fetches, if\nthe straight-line program is produced from its compacted form, then at every environment the\nprogram holds, at the remapped slot of every original node, exactly the value the original tape\ncomputes there. Constant leaves merged by compaction are covered, through the injectivity of\nFloat.toBits.\n\nThe compaction's denotation theorem, cseCompact_denotation_unconditional, composed with the\nprogram's denotation theorem for table-free tapes; the program's existence supplies the\ncompacted tape's evaluation.\n\nThe sharing recorder records the compacted tape. A carrier-generic model names a shared\nsub-expression once per use, so the recording carrier emits the expression tree and compaction\nshares it afterwards; the tree can be orders of magnitude larger than the model. The sharing\nrecorder looks each node up by cseCompact's key before appending it. For outputs related\noperation by operation at the two carriers and recorded from the empty tape at one tensor shape,\nboth recordings fail, or the sharing tape has the name, the operands and the stored value of\nevery node of the compacted unshared tape, in the same order, and its output ids are the\nremapped ids. Such a tape holds each key once (keys_distinct_of_fwd). That is the statement\nfor the unguarded entry recordRaw. The entry models use, record, also rejects a recording in\nwhich one input name holds two different tensors, which the unshared recorder accepts; for it\nthe statement is one-sided (shares_record_guarded): when record succeeds, the unshared\nrecording succeeds and the same agreement holds.\n\nA simulation between the sharing state and cseCompact's fold over the unshared tape, one node\nat a time (Rel.step), and a lemma that each elementwise recorder reads only its operands'\nvalues (raw_natural), which makes the lookup before running sound. The relation Shares is\npreserved by leaves, constants, + - * /, min, max, fmin, fmax, exp, log, abs,\nsqrt and the table fetch; the tactic tape_shares closes it for a concrete model.\n\nThe framework's own link. TorchLean's CUDA emitter: if emission of a kernel expression\nsucceeds, the generated source denotes the expression for any arithmetic and for buffer\ncontents read through bounds-checked loads. Program.of pairs each expression with a proof that\nit agrees with the Lean function it was recognized from. Compilation, memory and execution\nare outside the statement.\n\nA theorem of TorchLean, linked here because the table fetch's device kernel rests on it.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Metrology on a Verified Numerical Framework","header":"19.2. The trust chain, link by link","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Metrology-on-a-Verified-Numerical-Framework--The-trust-chain___-link-by-link"},"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L1-___-Two-bodies--Small-rigorous-improvement":{"contents":"* Question* Context change* Attribute change* Risk shown* PhysLib stage\n* There are two bodies now — whose mass is this?* Two particulars, Oscillator A and B, of one sort (harmonic oscillator); each black box contains one point-like body and its own wall spring. The oscillators do not interact. One name is one system; its records are states, configurations, or rival estimates to compare. R₁ and R₂ are distinct names even when parameters match. L2 opens each box into body and spring(s), and introduces their whole/part relations.* No new kinds: mass and stiffness share a rig-indexed oscillator bearer, allowing its k/m quotient.* B's mass can enter A's formula when only the kind is checked; one constant designation would collapse A and B into one system.* Stage 2: the kinded form carries its bearer.\n\nUnder L0's assumptions, each oscillator is a homogeneous, rigid, point-like body on one\nideal, massless spring attached to a fixed, infinitely massive wall, along a line. The\noscillator's mass is its body's mass, and its stiffness is its spring's. The oscillator\nis the whole characterized by ω₀, so k and m must carry the same oscillator index.\nThe two oscillators do not interact in L1. L2 revisits that assumption as it opens each\nbox into body and spring(s) and considers the pair as a whole.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L1 — Two bodies","header":"5.2.5. Small rigorous improvement","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L1-___-Two-bodies--Small-rigorous-improvement"},"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Using-the-library___-annotations-and-generated-indexes--The-generated-indexes--Kinds-and-their-algebra":{"contents":"The column that earns this table is Algebra. A kind's authored edges are the complete statement\nof what may be done with it — multiplied by what, divided into what, raised to what power — because\nthe calculus infers none of them. A kind whose Algebra cell is empty supports no arithmetic at all,\nand being able to see that is the point.\n\n* Kind* Identity* Scale* Algebra* Theorems* attenExponent* AVS attenuation exponent* ratio* produced (1)* paramB · vegetationIndex → attenExponentconsumed (1)* transcendental : attenExponent → attenuationK* attenuation_roles_distinct* attenuationK* AVS vegetation attenuation* ratio* produced (1)* transcendental : attenExponent → attenuationKconsumed (3)* jacB · attenuationK → jacB* attenuationK · paramC → paramC* attenuationK · reflectivity → reflectivity* attenuation_roles_distinct* backscatter* AVS backscatter coefficient σ⁰* ratio* produced (2)* paramA · vegetationIndex → backscatter* paramC · reflectivity → backscatter* * jacB* AVS backscatter times NDVI* ratio* produced (2)* jacB · attenuationK → jacB* jacBPartial · reflectivity → jacB* * jacBPartial* AVS NDVI times gain c* ratio* produced (1)* vegetationIndex · paramC → jacBPartialconsumed (1)* jacBPartial · reflectivity → jacB* * paramA* AVS gain a (σ⁰ per NDVI)* ratio* consumed (1)* paramA · vegetationIndex → backscatter* * paramB* AVS rate b (inverse NDVI)* ratio* produced (1)* pureNumber · paramB → paramBconsumed (1)* paramB · vegetationIndex → attenExponent* * paramC* AVS gain c (σ⁰ per reflectivity)* ratio* produced (1)* attenuationK · paramC → paramCconsumed (2)* paramC · reflectivity → backscatter* vegetationIndex · paramC → jacBPartial* * pureNumber* AVS pure number* ratio* consumed (2)* pureNumber · paramB → paramB* pureNumber · vegetationIndex → vegetationIndex* attenuation_roles_distinct* reflectivity* AVS surface reflectivity* ratio* produced (1)* attenuationK · reflectivity → reflectivityconsumed (2)* paramC · reflectivity → backscatter* jacBPartial · reflectivity → jacB* * vegetationIndex* AVS vegetation index* ratio* produced (1)* pureNumber · vegetationIndex → vegetationIndexconsumed (3)* paramB · vegetationIndex → attenExponent* paramA · vegetationIndex → backscatter* vegetationIndex · paramC → jacBPartial* * length* length* ratio* produced (1)* speed · time → length* length_is_speed_times_time* speed* speed* ratio* consumed (1)* speed · time → length* length_is_speed_times_time* time* time* ratio* consumed (1)* speed · time → length* length_is_speed_times_time* massFraction* mass fraction* ratio* * * volumeFraction* volume fraction* ratio* produced (1)* volumeFraction ± volumeFraction → volumeFraction* * dl* 1* ratio* produced (2)* dl · dl → dl* transcendental : dl → dl* pk, tk* height* height* ratio* * * length* length* ratio* * * width* width* ratio* * * mass* mass* ratio* * mass_extensive* lengthK* length* ratio* * * massK* mass* ratio* * instAssemblesGridSMassK* length* length* ratio* * hLen* mass* mass* ratio* * hMass* lengthKind* length* ratio* produced (1)* lengthKind ± lengthKind → lengthKind* hLen* displacement* displacement* ratio* produced (2)* displacement ± displacement → displacement* factor · displacement → displacementconsumed (1)* displacement · displacement → displacementSq* doubled_east, hDisp, hScale, hSquare, vector_add_comm* displacementSq* displacement squared* ratio* produced (1)* displacement · displacement → displacementSq* hSquare* factor* scale factor* ratio* consumed (1)* factor · displacement → displacement* hScale* backscatter* backscatter coefficient* ratio* * * conductivityKind* ionic conductivity* ratio* * * gwc* gravimetric water content* ratio* * * ndvi* vegetation index* ratio* * * reflBistatic* reflectivity* ratio* * * reflMonostatic* reflectivity* ratio* * * reflectivity* reflectivity* ratio* * cholRowQ_magnitudes* refractiveIndexKind* refractive index (n)* ratio* * * relPermKind* relative permittivity* ratio* * * relaxTimeKind* dielectric relaxation time* ratio* * * vwc* volumetric water content* ratio* * * bloodGroup* abo-blood-group* nominal* * * length* length* ratio* * * mass* mass* ratio* * * mohsHardness* mohs-hardness* ordinal* * * bloodGroup* abo-blood-group* nominal* * * length* length* ratio* * * mass* mass* ratio* * * height* height* ratio* * * length* length* ratio* * * width* width* ratio* * \n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Using the library: annotations and generated indexes\u0009The generated indexes","header":"35.10.1. Kinds and their algebra","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Using-the-library___-annotations-and-generated-indexes--The-generated-indexes--Kinds-and-their-algebra"},"/#deployment-template":{"contents":"A model document says what a boundary is. A deployment document says what deploying one\nadds, and the two are different enough that a single document doing both does neither\nwell. The model's reader wants to know what is proved and at which carrier; the\ndeployment's reader wants to know what will run, on what, against which contract, and what\nwould have to fail for the result to be rejected.\n\nThe separation is not editorial tidiness. Almost everything the deployment adds is\nunproved by construction: an image is built, not derived; a shard size is measured, not\ntheorem-backed; a queue is chosen. Mixing that material into the model document lets the\nmodel's green nodes lend it an assurance it does not have. Keeping it separate forces the\ndeployment to carry its own evidence, which is what this template enumerates.\n\nThe template has 17 rubrics: 9 discharged by a section that states the answer, 2 by a view generated from the compiled environment, 3 by a kernel-checked declaration, and 3 by a labeled measurement.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty","header":"37. Deploying a domain model: the deployment template","id":"/#deployment-template"}});